## Symptom

Effective machine sync interval is 4-6 minutes, not the configured 30s (live measured on xsm 2026-09-17: full-walk 4m08s and 5m31s; 93 heartbeats take ~77s; per-root last_ok spacing 5.5-5.8 min across 178 roots / 246 sessions - all serial HTTPS). Consequences: inbound relay DM latency approximates the pass period; only ~2.5 passes of margin under the staleness window (root e0eb9c317019 tripped 811s against a 769s window); heartbeat TTL headroom shrinks.

## Fix direction

Parallelize per-session ops within a root (bounded concurrency), and/or make the configured interval account for walk time (interval between pass starts rather than sleep after pass).

## Evidence

Live deployment audit 2026-09-17 (journal timings).

Resolution note 2026-09-18 (bl-fix-many, connector lane C5): PART (a) LANDED — machine-connector passes now start at fixed cadence (pass_start + interval, clamped >= 0; overrun restarts immediately; commit e6fd7aea; test_c2c_relay_b318_pass_cadence 2 tests green). PART (b) bounded cross-root concurrency SKIPPED AS UNSOUND under the current architecture, with analysis recorded in the C5 report: run_sync_once is a blocking function (per-root SIGALRM + nested Lwt_main.run per HTTP op — zero shared-loop await points, so an Lwt_pool/semaphore executes root syncs strictly serially with no wall-clock gain); OS-level forking collapses because Unix.alarm is one process-global timer (concurrent run_sync_once instances cancel each other's watchdog deadlines and restore each other's handlers, breaking the B307 force-exit-3), and wedge/strike/cooldown/rate-limit tables are in-parent state a forked child cannot report back via exit status. True cross-root parallelism requires promise-ifying the sync path (structural rewrite re-validating B292/B293/B297/B307/B313 bookkeeping) or a per-root subprocess watchdog design — a project, not a patch. Part (a) alone removes the unbounded drift, which was the user-visible defect.